<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=soybean">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soybean</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 7000 BCE</span> · Also known as soya bean, Glycine max</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The soybean is a species of legume native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, widely grown for its edible bean. It was domesticated from the wild soybean in north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, most likely in the region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>, between 6,000 and 9,000 years ago, and there is evidence for domestication between 7000 and 6600 BCE in China. Soybeans became an important crop by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tofu">tofu</a> and soy milk are traditional unfermented foods made from them. By the 18th century soybeans had been introduced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from China. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> now lead world production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soybean.jpg" alt="Soybean" width="330" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soybean.USDA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since the 1950s growth has been repeatedly interrupted by high inflation, debt crises and swings between protectionist and market-oriented policies, although agriculture, now led by <b>soybeans</b>, remains the main source of exports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Millet, barley and <b>soybeans</b> were the main crops, supplemented by hunting, stock-raising and iron-working.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Millet was the staple, together with wheat and <b>soybeans</b>, and many of the poems in the Book of Songs describe the agricultural year, feasting and the duties of nobles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>There is evidence that they tended groves of chestnut and lacquer trees and grew <b>soybean</b>, bottle gourd, hemp, perilla and adzuki beans, which places them somewhere between hunting-gathering and agriculture.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jomon-culture">Jōmon culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mohe, their tentative ancestors, were people of the multi-ethnic kingdom of Balhae: mainly sedentary farmers who grew <b>soybean</b>, wheat, millet and rice, kept pigs and rode reindeer.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jurchens">Jurchens · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 7000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>North-central China (between the Yellow and Huai rivers)</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The origin is debated: some evidence points to low-intensity cultivation across several places in East Asia before the Chinese lineage dominated. The first unambiguously domesticated, large-seeded soybeans come from Daundong in Korea, and the oldest preserved modern-looking ones from about 1000 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Agriculture<span>c. 10,000 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/tofu">Tofu<span>Han dynasty</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=soybean">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
